**New analysis:** I tested whether the 09-24 identity metric detects the known 2026-06-07 step, when the prompt the model received changed. It does:
- Pairs of sessions on opposite sides of the step score clearly lower than pairs on the same side at the same distance. On McNugget it's 0.36 against about 0.52.
- Sessions are assigned to the correct side of the step 99–100% of the time; chance is about 50%.
- The step is about twice as large as the difference between the two lines. The two lines are also distinguishable before the step.

My first run filtered sessions by the number of questions and put the pre-step line separation at chance. Filtering on the exact tutor wording is the right method, since the wording changed before the step. The write-up records both runs. This answers the objection that the metric is too blunt to notice changes to what a line is given. It doesn't show whether the metric would catch the smaller scramble, and it doesn't favour either account. What blocks the control now is the design question above, not the measurement.

